# Drop-in for the RELAY: /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10-buendia-tunnel.conf   (chmod 600)
#
# ⚠️ sshd takes the FIRST value obtained for most keywords, and Ubuntu's
#    `Include /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf` sits at the TOP of sshd_config — so a drop-in
#    wins over the main file. Appending to the bottom of sshd_config does NOT (measured:
#    an appended AllowTcpForwarding was silently ignored).

# The relay used for the spike restricts logins (AllowUsers ubuntu). A tunnel account must be
# added explicitly or it is rejected before any key is considered.
AllowUsers ubuntu tunnel

# ⚠️ These ClientAlive settings are NOT what fixes a dead tunnel. Measured: with a blackholed
# peer (the real field failure) sshd's keepalive probes sit unacknowledged in the socket send
# queue, the write never fails, and the session is NOT reaped -- 30 x 2 was still holding the
# port at 405s. They are kept only for the tidy cases (peer answers, or sends a RST).
#
# THE setting that decides recovery is the KERNEL retransmission budget, and it lives in sysctl,
# not here.  798s (stock) -> 32s with:
#     net.ipv4.tcp_retries2 = 6      # /etc/sysctl.d/10-buendia-relay.conf
# It is system-wide, so the relay must be a DEDICATED instance, not a shared box.
Match User tunnel
    ClientAliveInterval 30
    ClientAliveCountMax 2
    PermitTTY no
    X11Forwarding no
    AllowAgentForwarding no
